  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 350 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry today, with only isolated showers/storms\nfor the SW 1/2 of the area, then widely scattered showers/storms\nthis evening across southern VA and NE NC. Higher chances are \nexpected Friday afternoon-Friday night with a wave of low pressure\nriding along a weakening frontal boundary.\n\nCurrent WX analysis indicates W-NW flow aloft with a subtle sfc\ntrough stretching SW from southern New England into the local\narea. Dew pts are slightly higher than 24 hrs ago, mostly in the\nupper 60s to lower 70s. An area of mid level clouds is moving\nthrough the northern 1/2 of the FA and will slide offshore and\ndiminish after sunrise. Overall, expect a mostly sunny day with\nSCT aftn cumulus. Model soundings show fairly minimal instability\ntoday despite higher temperatures and humidity compared to \nyesterday. CAMs are not very excited about storms developing\nwhich makes sense given the minimal instability, weak forcing\nand low shear. We are not in any SVR or ERO risk today. as most\nareas climb back into the lower 90s. Heat indices across the \nregion will range from the mid 90s across the north to around \n100F in the south, with some of the typical areas along the \nAlbemarle sound into the lower 100s. May see the highest PoPs\n(up to ~30%) this evening across NE NC and far SE VA. \n\nOn Friday, the models have actually diverged a bit with respect\nto the evolution of the frontal boundary interacting with an \nupper level shortwave passing through. the 00Z/13 NAM is noted \nas a significant outlier, developing an anomalously strong sfc \nlow and indicated a strong MCS moving through later in the day. \nThe ECMWF has trended a bit to the N with instability as well, \nbut still nothing like the NAM, The GFS and CAMs show the \npotential for rather disorganized showers/storms. Overall, this \nleads to a high degree of uncertainty for the short range. If \nHave generally broad-brushed PoPs in the high chc range (40-50%)\nfrom Friday afternoon through Friday night. A Marginal SVR risk\nis in place, as well as a Marginal ERO- basically highly \ndependent on instability. If we get the instability, then a \nmore organized wind threat and heavy rain threat could \nmaterialize, but as has been the case the past several days, \nthese MCS's tracking in the W-NW flow aloft have been poorly \nhandled by the guidance. Warm/very humid ahead of the storms \nFriday, with highs low-mid 90s NE NC, and in the mid-upper 80s \nto the N. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry and slightly less humid Saturday,\nseasonable Sunday with a few afternoon showers/storms.\n\nSaturday looks mostly dry and fairly pleasant with highs mostly\nin the mid to upper 80s in the wake of the cold front, though \nmore clouds and an isolated showers/storm will be possible in\nthe piedmont and across NE NC. The upper ridge starts to build \nback into the region Saturday night/Sunday, and some of the \nmodels even depict showers Saturday night. Scattered shower and \nisolated thunderstorms will return to the forecast on Sunday, \nalong with highs back around 90F for most of the area. The \nexpansive upper ridge across the southern U.S. will finally \nstart to break down and flow aloft will become more zonal. The \ncurrent forecast currently has the normal diurnally driven \nafternoon/evening isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms. Monday looks hot with aft storms possible. Heat\nAdvisories may be needed across at least the Se 1/2 of the CWA.",
